From c6dd0e4ad538b0637677e311f2fc25afb12096a6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cpkt9762 <cpkt9762@163.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 17:06:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(store): route CJK search per term instead of per query
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Forward-port of fork patches 0001 and 0002, reduced to what upstream still
gets wrong.

Upstream adopted the trigram tokenizer in #924, so the original half of this
patch -- switching the FTS tables off unicode61 -- is gone. All four
tokenize='trigram' declarations are upstream's now. Upstream also fixed the
rune-counting bug the fork PR had warned about: hasShortFTSTerm uses
utf8.RuneCountInString, not len.

What upstream does with a short term is send the entire query to
buildSearchLIKEQuery. That is correct for recall but it is an unranked
full-table scan: rank is hardcoded 0.0 and ordering is by updated_at, so
BM25, the title/content weighting and the new pinned/recency/stability
composite ranking are all discarded. One short term anywhere in the query is
enough to trigger it, and in Chinese a two-character word beside a longer one
is the ordinary way to search, so in practice most real queries land there.

Routing per term keeps the long half on trigram MATCH and intersects the
short half on the base table, so the result keeps a real relevance score.
TestSearchCJK_MixedLengthQuery now asserts rank != 0 for exactly this reason;
reverting to whole-query fallback fails it.

Two places where the fork's original shape did not survive contact:

- "any" match mode cannot use this. A short term under OR semantics has to be
  able to add rows the FTS match missed, and a predicate on the joined table
  can only remove them. That combination keeps upstream's LIKE scan, which is
  what TestShortTermFallbackEscapesAndPreservesFiltersAndMatchModes pins.
- The all-short-terms case also stays on upstream's LIKE path rather than
  getting a private one, but termPredicate now makes that path script-aware:
  a short Latin term matches on GLOB word boundaries so "go" stops hitting
  "golang" and "algorithm", while CJK and any term of three runes or more
  keep plain substring matching. Upstream's column coverage (including type
  and project) and its escaping tests are untouched.

Counting runes rather than bytes remains the single most dangerous detail: a
two-character Chinese word is six bytes, so len() classifies it as long, it
goes to MATCH, trigram cannot index it, and the query silently returns
nothing. Mutation-checked -- swapping in len() fails four CJK tests including
"expected 2 rows containing 代理 inside a longer run, got 0".

Full suite: failing-package set identical to the pristine upstream/main
baseline.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
